  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14208490/assets/afbe02b63ca4d45ae4d1759d7fa452b57a8b43f073d08b1368f51472e2e4ea8b.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the dock and the boat,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the fisherman's hands and the ground, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the fisherman's hands and the boat mooring.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $x,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=3\\,\\text{ft}$ and $y=4\\,\\text{ft }.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & ={3}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =9+16 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =25 \\\\ z & =5\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=-3\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{h},$\n\n- the distance between the fisherman's hands and the ground remains the same, so $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(3)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(4)(0) & =2(5)(-3) \\\\ 6\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-30 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the dock and the boat is decreasing at a rate of $5\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.$",
